---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Arrow from "../components/Arrow.astro";
import { GITHUB } from "../lib/site";
// the app's About > Privacy (pktito-app, ui/locales/en.json, privacy.*) says
// the same of the app; the two change together, and keep the date
---
<Base title="Privacy · pktito" description="What pktito sends where: the app transcribes on your computer or phone, and a model download and this site are the only things that reach the internet.">
  <main class="wrap">
    <section class="page-head">
      <h1 class="display">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="measure">pktito is local transcription software: it turns speech into text on your computer or phone. Your media files are not uploaded to cloud servers, and the app has no accounts, ads or tracking. This page says what does reach the internet, and what happens to it.</p>
      <p class="measure legal-date">Last updated October 26, 2026</p>
    </section>
    <section class="part">
      <h2 class="lead">Downloading a model</h2>
      <p class="measure">The app downloads AI models from models.pktito.net, hosted on a content delivery network (Cloudflare or bunny.net). The server logs your IP address, the time, the file requested and a standard user-agent string, but no name, account or device identifier.</p>
      <p class="measure">These logs are used to keep the service secure and to count downloads in aggregate. We don't use them to identify you, combine them with other data, or sell or share them. To skip the download, use a model file you already have: choose “Open a model file…” in the app.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="part">
      <h2 class="lead">This website</h2>
      <p class="measure">pktito.net sets no cookies and runs no analytics. Cloudflare serves it, and like any web host it handles your IP address and browser details to deliver the pages and protect the site from abuse. When an installer download starts, the site adds one to that file’s count for the day. It keeps nothing else: no addresses, nothing that tells one visitor from another.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="part">
      <h2 class="lead">Links</h2>
      <p class="measure">Links in the app and on this site go to other sites, such as GitHub and Hugging Face. Their own privacy policies apply there.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="part">
      <h2 class="lead">Your data</h2>
      <p class="measure">Everything the app keeps is on your computer, in its data folder: your settings, the models and the recent transcripts. Clearing the recent list deletes its transcripts. Uninstalling pktito leaves its data folder for you to delete. pktito from the Microsoft Store keeps its settings, models and recent transcripts with the app instead, and uninstalling it deletes them.</p>
      <p class="measure">On Android, the app keeps its settings, its models and the transcript open with the app, out of Android’s backups, and uninstalling it deletes them. It opens only the recordings you choose, open with it or share to it, saves only where you choose, and asks for no access to your storage. A transcript you share goes to the app you pick.</p>
      <p class="measure">We hold nothing about you to look up, correct or delete.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="part">
      <h2 class="lead">Changes and questions</h2>
      <p class="measure">If this policy changes, this page changes with it, under a new date, and the app’s copy comes with its next update. Ask questions by email, at <a class="pk-link" id="ask-mail" data-user="peter.hebert" data-domain="pktito.net">peter.hebert at pktito.net</a>, or <Arrow href={`${GITHUB}/issues`}>on GitHub</Arrow></p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <script is:inline>
    // the address put together here rather than written in the page, out of
    // the way of scrapers that read pages for mailto: links and a@b
    {
      const a = document.getElementById("ask-mail");
      const to = a.dataset.user + "@" + a.dataset.domain;
      a.href = "mailto:" + to;
      a.textContent = to;
    }
  </script>
</Base>
